@meta
id: 16-09
title: Two views of one file
chapter: 16
type: lesson
skills: split-window, window-nav
par: 215
minutes: 7
vscode: split editor right, then open the same file in it, and watch the two copies argue
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
A window is a view of a buffer, and nothing says there can be only one.
`:sp` splits the current window in two, one above the other, both
showing the buffer you were in. Scroll one to the bottom and the other
stays where it was. Edit in either and the change is in both instantly,
because there is only one buffer; the windows are two cameras pointed
at it.

This is for the file that is too long to see at once. Here, `cancel`
at the bottom of the provider already calls `this.emailService.send`,
because someone wrote the call and forgot the plumbing. Split the
window, put the bottom one on the call so you can see the name being
used, and in the top one add the plumbing:

  1. An import, directly under the `PaymentService` import:

         import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';

  2. A constructor parameter, directly under the `PaymentService` one:

         @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,

`:wqa` writes the buffer once and closes both windows.

@keys
| `:sp` | split horizontally; the new window is on top and has the cursor |
| `Ctrl-W s` | the same, as a chord |
| `Ctrl-W j` | move to the window below |
| `Ctrl-W k` | move to the window above |
| `:wqa` | write, close every window, quit |

@hints
`:sp`, then `Ctrl-W j` and `G` so the lower window shows the end of
`cancel`. `Ctrl-W k` back up; the upper window is still at the top of
the file.
--
The import: `/payment.service`, `o`, type it, `Esc`. The parameter:
`/PaymentService)`, `o`, type it, `Esc`. Both lines are long; that is
the cost of the ticket, not of the split.
--
`:wqa`. A plain `:wq` would only close one of the two windows, and vim
would still be open.

@solution
    :sp<Enter>  Ctrl-W j  G  Ctrl-W k
    /payment.service<Enter>  o import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service'; <Esc>
    /PaymentService)<Enter>  o @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService, <Esc>
    :wqa<Enter>

Around a hundred and ninety keystrokes, nearly all of them the two
lines the ticket asked for. The split and the two window moves are
ten.

What you get for those ten is the thing VS Code makes awkward: a second
view of the same file, live, no second copy, no "which one did I edit".
Two windows on one buffer is the standard way to write a call at the
bottom while reading the signature at the top, or to keep a test's
setup in view while writing the assertion below it.

The other thing to notice: `:sp` is the first command in this chapter
that changed the layout without changing the list. `:ls` after it
still shows one buffer. Windows come and go; the buffer is what you
are editing.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });
  }

  public async ship(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'PENDING') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_PENDING');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' },
    });
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    await this.emailService.send({
      customerId: order.customerId,
      template: 'order-cancelled',
    });

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });
  }

  public async ship(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'PENDING') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_PENDING');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' },
    });
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    await this.emailService.send({
      customerId: order.customerId,
      template: 'order-cancelled',
    });

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}
